==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
A borrowing of {{uder|en|la|[[sua]] [[sponte]]||at its own [[prompting]]; by its own [[impulse]]; of its own [[free will]]}}

===Phrase===
{{head|en|phrase|head=sua sponte}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} Without prompting or suggestion; on its own initiative.
#* '''1878''', "[[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition/Decretals|Decretals]]" in the [[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition|''Encyclopædia Britannica'', 9th ed.]], [[:s:Encyclopædia Britannica, Ninth Edition/Volume VII|Vol. VII]], p. 22:
#*: [[decretal|Decretals]]... ought, properly speaking, to be distinguished, on the one hand from [[constitution]]s... enacted by the Pope '''''sua sponte''''' without reference to any particular case, and on the other hand from [[rescript]]s... which apply only to special circumstances or individuals, and constitute no general precedent. But this nomenclature is not strictly observed.

====Usage notes====
* Usually used when the court issues an order or otherwise acts without being petitioned to do so.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|suo motu}}, {{l|en|nostra sponte}}, {{l|en|motu proprio}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|off one's own bat}}

===References===
* Black's Law Dictionary 10th Edition (2014)

